>HMAC - stands for Hash Based Message Authentication Code. 
>Its a MAC type of authentication required for executing a cryptographic hash function.
>It ensures data integrity and authenticity.
>Uses a hash function and a secret key.
>Use cases are HTTPS, FTPS, SFTP.
>The cryptographic hash function maybe MD-5, SHA-1 or SHA-256.
>HMAC uses symmetric key while Digital Signatures use an asymmetric (two different keys)

Working
>HMAC provides the client and the server with a shared key that is only known to them as is used to decrypt the message.
>Formula  HMAC= hashFunc(secret key + message)
>For security purposes the messages are hashed in two different steps.
>i.e HMAC key (key, message) = H(mod1(key) || mod2(key) || message)

Advantages
1. Ideal for high performance systems
2. Provides a higher security compared to digital signatures.
3. Used in administrations where public key systems are prohibited.

Disadvantages.
1. Uses a shared key which may lead to non-repudition.
2. Securely managing and distributing keys can be challenging.
3. Security relies on the length of the secret key
4. Strength relies on the chosen hash function.

Applications.
1. Verification of emails addresses during account creation.
2. Authentication of form data that is sent to the client browser then submitted back.
3. Used for Internet of Things since its cheaper.
4. Needed for password resetting where a link is sent.
5. Can take a long message and convert it into a fixed length using message digest.
